---
/**
 * /about/independence: who pays for the site, what the author takes and does
 * not take, and what he may sell. Every sentence is the owner's to confirm;
 * the "Last reviewed" date moves only when he re-confirms the whole page. Any
 * paid product or service related to the site is listed here before it launches.
 */
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { gitDate } from '../../lib/reading';

const reviewed = '2026-11-18';
const reportForm = `${site.github}/issues/new?template=report-an-error.yml`;

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'who-pays', text: 'Who pays' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'what-i-do-not-take', text: 'What I do not take' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'whose-project-this-is', text: 'Whose project this is' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'what-i-may-sell', text: 'What I may sell' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'who-else-is-involved', text: 'Who else is involved' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'how-the-lab-frameworks-are-treated', text: 'How the lab frameworks are treated' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'how-to-challenge-this', text: 'How to challenge this' },
];

const title = 'Independence and funding';
const summary =
  'Who pays for this site, what I take and do not take, and what I may sell.';
const description =
  'Who pays for AI Governance Engineer, what its author takes and does not take, what he may sell in this field, and how to challenge any of it.';
const updated = gitDate('src/pages/about/independence.astro');
---

<Doc
  title={title}
  summary={summary}
  description={description}
  crumbs={[{ label: 'About', href: '/about' }, { label: title }]}
  headings={headings}
  updated={updated}
  navGroup="about"
  image="/og/about.png"
>
  <p>
    This page says who pays for this site and what ties I have to the field it covers. Each
    statement on it can be challenged.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-pays">Who pays</h2>
  <p>I pay for this site myself: the domain, the hosting and the tools.</p>

  <h2 id="what-i-do-not-take">What I do not take</h2>
  <p>
    The site has no sponsors, no grants, no advertising, no affiliate links and no paid placement.
    For this site I take no money, access or other support from AI labs, vendors, standards bodies
    or certification schemes, and the site is not affiliated with any of them. The pages that rely
    on a scheme
    say so too: the <a href="/controls">open control profiles</a> are not affiliated with AIUC, ISO,
    NIST or the Cloud Security Alliance, and the <a href="/for/aigp">AIGP map</a> is not affiliated
    with or endorsed by the IAPP.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whose-project-this-is">Whose project this is</h2>
  <p>
    This is a personal project, written on my own time. It speaks for no employer, and nothing on it
    discloses an employer's internal detail.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-i-may-sell">What I may sell</h2>
  <p>
    I work in this field and may be paid for work in it: advisory work, training or software.
    Nothing is sold on this site. If I offer a paid product or service related to what this site
    covers, it will be listed on this page before it launches. As of the date below, nothing is
    listed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-else-is-involved">Who else is involved</h2>
  <p>
    I am the sole author of the Body of Knowledge and the site; the Thesis is co-authored with
    Aurélie Pols. No review of a chapter or dataset has been completed yet, and the
    <a href="/about/contributors">contributors page</a> asks every reviewer to disclose any
    commercial interest in a vendor, product or standard the reviewed text names.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-the-lab-frameworks-are-treated">How the lab frameworks are treated</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/resources/frontier-framework-changes">frontier framework changes</a> page describes
    the labs' published documents; it makes no claim about how any lab applies its framework, and it
    does not rate any change. Across the site, a claim carried only by a vendor is attributed to that
    vendor, and tools are named only as examples of a category, never as a recommendation (see
    <a href="/about/methodology#how-sources-are-chosen">how sources are chosen</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-challenge-this">How to challenge this</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is wrong, or a page reads as favouring someone, use the
    <a href={reportForm} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">report an error</a> form or write
    to <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. The
    <a href="/about/methodology#corrections-and-contributions">corrections process</a> and
    <a href="/contribute">how to contribute</a> say how reports are handled. Except where a page
    notes otherwise, the text and data are licensed <a href={site.licenseUrl}>{site.license}</a>, and
    the <a href={site.github}>repository</a> is public, so anyone can check them or fork them.
  </p>

  <p class="reviewed">Last reviewed: <time datetime={reviewed}>{reviewed}</time>.</p>
</Doc>

<style>
  /* Authored h2/p in Doc's .prose well: :global(.prose) lifts these over
     prose.css, as on /about/methodology. */
  :global(.prose) > h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-h2);
  }

  .reviewed {
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
